# Model Registry — engine model catalogue

- The "Add Model" browse is served by **`nvpair-engine-manager`** over the
  **`engine:catalog`** JSON-RPC method (`services/nvpair-engine-manager/catalog.go`).
  It lives in the backend because both front ends need it: the desktop app's
  Add Model modal and the terminal interface's model browser. There is **no
  shared GGUF registry** — llama.cpp has the one curated source, selected by an
  engine's manifest `family`; an engine with none is an error, not an empty list.
- **llama.cpp**: a **live fetch** of the top 50 GGUF repositories for each
  approved publisher (`ggml-org`, `bartowski`, and `unsloth`). It is
  **searchable**: `engine:catalog` takes an optional `query`, which
  searches up to 50 matches across all public publishers, each query cached on
  its own (the 20 most recently used are kept). Both paths reject unsafe,
  private, gated, non-generative, and non-pull-ready repos. Every result is an
  exact `owner/repository:Q4_K_M` pull id; other quantizations are not listed.
  The reply says `searchable: true`. **Never log the user's search text.**
- The llama.cpp browse list and each llama.cpp query share one cache behaviour:
  6-hour TTL, a coalescing guard so concurrent callers share one request, and a
  failure backoff so a dead upstream is not re-dialled on every call.
- Results normalize to `CatalogModel`, which the desktop maps to the shared
  `EngineHubModel` (`desktop/src/shared/types/engine-api.ts`).
- **The catalogue is filtered for the machine the models will install on**, not
  the one serving it. `engine:catalog` takes an optional `platform` and `arch`
  (GOOS and GOARCH; omitting both means the server's own) and echoes the target
  it was asked for. A named platform without an arch leaves the arch unknown
  rather than borrowing the server's. `filterForTarget` drops rows marked
  `appleOnly` (MLX quantizations, which only install on Apple Silicon) for any
  target but `darwin`/`arm64`; GGUF has no machine-locked quantizations, so the
  llama.cpp list is not filtered. A client driving a peer should say which
  peer; a client that cannot determine it must tell the operator which machine
  the list applies to rather than presenting it as universal.
- **Frame size matters here.** The reply is a single JSON-RPC line. Every hop
  on its path shares `jsonrpc.WorkerFrameBytes`, because an over-long line is a
  terminal read error that silently closes the peer while the child keeps
  running. `TestLlamaCPPCatalogFitsInAFrame` in `catalog_test.go` fails if the
  largest browse list outgrows the frame; **filter or paginate rather than
  raising the cap again**.
- The desktop relays through
  `desktop/src/electron/service-bridge/model-catalog.ts`, which exposes
  `getEngineHubModels(engineType, query?)` and `warmEngineHubs()` (warms the
  llama.cpp catalogue). The `engine:search-hub` handler in `empty-handlers.ts`
  calls the former; the `overview:ready` handler in `window.ipc.ts` calls the
  latter. Warm on renderer-ready, **not** on service connect: a catalog fetch
  started before the window has painted competes with the renderer's own load,
  and a hanging one leaves an unpainted window behind.
- Both front ends fetch an engine's full catalog once (cached per-engine) and
  filter **locally** as the user types. The desktop renderer
  (`src/ui/components/ModelHub/`) sorts client-side by Updated / Name / Size;
  Enter/Search sends an explicit query and merges the response with matching
  listed rows. The terminal interface's browser sends `/` as a query and shows
  its results. Pulling goes through the engine manager (`engine:pull-model` →
  `pull_model`); the catalogue only produces the pull-ready id.
